{
 "evidenceId": "CAMERA-VIZIER-EXTRACTS-01",
 "scope": "Cone-search responses for the 17 ObjectX.HTL target positions (SR 0.25 deg: the widest CCD query cone, 0.228 deg on the 0.4 m telescope, plus about 78 arcsec of pointing tolerance), NOMAD I/297 and USNO-B1 I/284, saved byte for byte. Only RA, DEC and SR were sent.",
 "approval": "AGENTS.md 2026-10-27: CDS/VizieR NOMAD/USNO-B1 static extracts (undeclared licence documented), extended by the user to the official CfA mirror (2026-10-27) and to every official VizieR mirror (2026-10-29, e.g. vizier.idia.ac.za, which answered when CDS and CfA did not), provided each extract records its serving host.",
 "hostPolicy": "Each extract comes whole from one host (host field); the server request INFO names the same host. Hosts are never mixed within one extract.",
 "recorder": "/home/dobleuber/Documents/Codex/2026-10-17/task-2/analysis/front-c5w/tools/record.sh and build_extracts.py (Front C, with the mirror list of Front C5W)",
 "citations": [
  "CDS/VizieR, doi:10.26093/cds/vizier",
  "NOMAD: Zacharias N. et al. 2004, AAS 205, 48.15 (2004AAS...205.4815Z)",
  "USNO-B1.0: Monet D.G. et al. 2003, AJ 125, 984"
 ],
 "licence": "VizieR declares no explicit licence for I/297 or I/284 (rights_uri https://cds.unistra.fr/vizier-org/licences_vizier.html). Probably US-government USNO works; uncertain.",
 "nomadJHK": "NOMAD J/H/K (used by the IR camera, docs/CAMERA-IR.md) originate in 2MASS: Skrutskie M.F. et al. 2006, AJ 131, 1163 (UMass, IPAC/Caltech; NASA, NSF). The same I/297 extracts serve the CCD and IR cameras (IR query cone <= 0.19 deg).",
 "moving-targets": "Rows for the three moving ObjectX.HTL targets (asteroids) are cones at their HTL positions; the asteroids move, so these extracts serve only pointings near those positions.",
 "naming": "Each extract is named by catalog, cone centre (degrees, 4 decimals) and radius, never by the Object X target it serves, so the camera source line cannot reveal the target.",
